              engineering strategies have been developed to aid in cartilage
              regeneration. Tissue engineering approaches often require the
              integration of cells, biomaterials, and growth factors to direct
              and support tissue formation. A variety of cell types have been
              isolated from adipose, bone marrow, muscle, and skin tissue to
              promote cartilage regeneration. The interaction of cells with each
              other and with their surrounding environment has been shown to
              play a key role in cartilage engineering. In tissue engineering
              approaches, biomaterials are commonly used to provide an initial
              framework for cell recruitment and proliferation and tissue
              formation. Modifications of the properties of biomaterials, such
              as creating sites for cell binding, altering their physicochemical
              characteristics, and regulating the delivery of growth factors,
