  abstract = "A VAMAS (Versailles Project on Advanced Materials and Standards)
              interlaboratory study for desorption electrospray ionization mass
              spectrometry (DESI MS) measurements has been conducted with the
              involvement of 20 laboratories from 10 countries. Participants
              were provided with an analytical protocol and two reference
              samples: a thin layer of Rhodamine B and double-sided adhesive
              tape, each on separate glass slides. The studies comprised
              acquisition of positive ion mass spectra in predetermined m/z
              ranges. No sample preparation was required. Results for Rhodamine
              B show that very consistent craters may be generated. However,
              inadequacies of the spray and sample stage designs often lead to
              variable crater shapes. The average repeatability for Rhodamine B
              is 50\%. Yet, repeatabilities better than 20\% can be achieved.
              Rhodamine B proved to be an excellent reference sample to check
              the sample erosion crater, the sample stage movement and memory
              effects. Adhesive tape samples show that their average absolute
              intensity repeatability is 30\% and the relative repeatability is
              9\%. The constancy of these spectra from relative intensities
              gives day-to-day average relative repeatabilities of 31\%, three
              times worse than the short-term repeatability. Significant
              differences in the spectra from different laboratories arise from
              the different adventitious adducts observed or from contaminants
              that may cause the higher day-to-day variations. It is thought
