              biomaterials and the release of fluorescent soluble by-products
              were confirmed as accurate methods to quantify degradation. It was
              demonstrated that increasing rates of the selected biomaterials'
              degradation led to a decrease in cell proliferation and
              concurrently an increase in osteogenic matrix production. A novel
              turnover index (TI), which directly describes the effect of
              degradation of a biomaterial on cell behaviour, was calculated.
              Lower TIs for proliferation and high TIs for osteogenic marker
              production were observed on faster degrading biomaterials,
              indicating that these biomaterials supported an upregulation of
              osteogenic markers. This TI was further validated using an ex vivo
