  journal  = "Cold Spring Harb. Protoc.",
  volume   =  2008,
  number   =  8,
  pages    = "db.prot4991",
  abstract = "Cryosections are rapidly and relatively easily prepared prior to
              fixation, and they provide a good system for visualizing fine
              details of the cell. Although cryosections are physically less
              stable than paraffin- or resin-embedded sections, they are
              generally superior for the preservation of antigenicity and
              therefore the detection of antigens by microscopy. The preparation
              of cryosections does not involve the dehydration steps typical of
              other sectioning methods, and, furthermore, sectioning, labeling,
              and observation of specimens can usually be carried out in one
              day. In general, the sample is frozen quickly in either isopentane
              or liquid nitrogen. (Small samples such as cells and small tissues
              may be mixed in a slurry of an inert support medium such as
              optimal cutting temperature [OCT] compound before freezing). Rapid
              freezing reduces ice crystal formation and minimizes morphological
              damage. Frozen sections may be used for a variety of procedures,
              including immunochemistry, enzymatic detection, and in situ
              hybridization. A protocol for cryosectioning is presented here.",
