              metabolism. OXPHOS metabolism, which relies predominantly on
              mitochondrial respiration, exhibits fine-tuned regulation of
              respiratory chain complexes and enhanced antioxidant response or
              detoxification capacity. CRITICAL ISSUES: OXPHOS-dependent cancer
              cells use alternative oxidizable substrates, such as glutamine and
              fatty acids. The diversity of carbon substrates fueling neoplastic
              cells is indicative of metabolic heterogeneity, even within tumors
              sharing the same clinical diagnosis. Metabolic switch supports
              cancer cell stemness and their bioenergy-consuming functions, such
              as proliferation, survival, migration, and invasion. Moreover,
              reactive oxygen species-induced mitochondrial metabolism and
              nutrient availability are important for interaction with tumor
