  author   = "Race, Alan Mark and Römpp, Andreas",
  journal  = "Anal. Chem.",
  abstract = "Open data formats are key to facilitating data sharing,
              integration and interoperability. The imzML format has drastically
              improved these aspects of mass spectrometry imaging data, however
              compatibility issues still occasionally occur. Here we present a
              validation tool for data stored in both imzML and the HUPO-PSI
              mass spectrometery counterpart, mzML, to identify any deviations
              from the published (i)mzML standard which could cause issues for
              the user when visualising or processing data. The tool is released
              in two forms, a graphical user interface for ease of use, which
              also allows editing of the metadata included within the (i)mzML
              files, and a command line version to fit into existing workflows
              and pipelines. When known issues are encountered, such as the
              presence of negative values for the offset at which data is
              stored, the validator resolves the issue automatically upon
              saving. We also present a means of performing conditional
              validation on the metadata within (i)mzML files, where
              user-defined rules are validated against dependent on whether
              specific metadata are present (or not). For example, if the MALDI
              term is present, then additional rules related to MALDI (such as
              the requirement of inclusion of laser parameters) can be validated
              against. This enables a flexible and more thorough automated
              validation of (i)mzML data. Such a system is necessary for
              validating against more comprehensive sets of metadata such as
