  journal  = "Science",
  volume   =  353,
  number   =  6307,
  abstract = "Tumors comprise functionally diverse subpopulations of cells with
              distinct proliferative potential. Here, we show that dynamic
              epigenetic states defined by the linker histone H1.0 determine
              which cells within a tumor can sustain the long-term cancer
              growth. Numerous cancer types exhibit high inter- and intratumor
              heterogeneity of H1.0, with H1.0 levels correlating with tumor
              differentiation status, patient survival, and, at the single-cell
              level, cancer stem cell markers. Silencing of H1.0 promotes
              maintenance of self-renewing cells by inducing derepression of
              megabase-sized gene domains harboring downstream effectors of
              oncogenic pathways. Self-renewing epigenetic states are not
