              involved in the pathogenesis of cancer, but which nutrients
              support the inappropriate growth of intact malignant tumors is
              incompletely understood. Amino acids are essential nutrients for
              many cancer cells that can be obtained through the scavenging and
              catabolism of extracellular protein via macropinocytosis. In
              particular, macropinocytosis can be a nutrient source for
              pancreatic cancer cells, but it is not fully understood how the
              tumor environment influences metabolic phenotypes and whether
              macropinocytosis supports the maintenance of amino acid levels
              within pancreatic tumors. Here we utilize miniaturized plasma
              exchange to deliver labeled albumin to tissues in live mice, and
              we demonstrate that breakdown of albumin contributes to the supply
              of free amino acids in pancreatic tumors. We also deliver albumin
              directly into tumors using an implantable microdevice, which was
              adapted and modified from ref. 9. Following implantation, we
              directly observe protein catabolism and macropinocytosis in situ
