  number   =  1,
  pages    = "27--47",
  abstract = "Tumorigenesis is dependent on the reprogramming of cellular
              metabolism as both direct and indirect consequence of oncogenic
              mutations. A common feature of cancer cell metabolism is the
              ability to acquire necessary nutrients from a frequently
              nutrient-poor environment and utilize these nutrients to both
              maintain viability and build new biomass. The alterations in
              intracellular and extracellular metabolites that can accompany
              cancer-associated metabolic reprogramming have profound effects on
              gene expression, cellular differentiation, and the tumor
              microenvironment. In this Perspective, we have organized known
              cancer-associated metabolic changes into six hallmarks: (1)
              deregulated uptake of glucose and amino acids, (2) use of
              opportunistic modes of nutrient acquisition, (3) use of
