  volume   =  51,
  number   =  5,
  pages    = "1262--1277",
  abstract = "Multiparameter tissue imaging enables analysis of cell-cell
              interactions in situ, the cellular basis for tissue structure, and
              novel cell types that are spatially restricted, giving clues to
              biological mechanisms behind tissue homeostasis and disease. Here,
              we streamlined and simplified the multiplexed imaging method
              CO-Detection by indEXing (CODEX) by validating 58 unique
              oligonucleotide barcodes that can be conjugated to antibodies. We
              showed that barcoded antibodies retained their specificity for
              staining cognate targets in human tissue. Antibodies were
              visualized one at a time by adding a fluorescently labeled
              oligonucleotide complementary to oligonucleotide barcode, imaging,
              stripping, and repeating this cycle. With this we developed a
              panel of 46 antibodies that was used to stain five human lymphoid
              tissues: three tonsils, a spleen, and a LN. To analyze the data
              produced, an image processing and analysis pipeline was developed
