  abstract = "Spatially resolved transcriptomics technologies enable the
              measurement of transcriptome information while retaining the
              spatial context at the regional, cellular or sub-cellular level.
              While previous computational methods have relied on gene
              expression information alone for clustering single-cell
              populations, more recent methods have begun to leverage spatial
              location and histology information to improve cell clustering and
              cell-type identification. In this study, using seven
              semi-synthetic datasets with real spatial locations, simulated
              gene expression and histology images as well as ground truth
              cell-type labels, we evaluate 15 clustering methods based on
              clustering accuracy, robustness to data variation and input
              parameters, computational efficiency, and software usability. Our
              analysis demonstrates that even though incorporating the
              additional spatial and histology information leads to increased
              accuracy in some datasets, it does not consistently improve
