  journal  = "ChemRxiv",
  abstract = "Laser desorption ionisation (LDI) is generally considered to be an
              inferior ionisation modality to matrix assisted LDI (MALDI),
              providing information solely on lipids with low sensitivity. The
              current study demonstrates that the combination of ambient LDI
              with in-source surface-induced declustering provides sensitivity
              and chemical coverage comparable to MALDI. The setup was
              characterised for infrared laser desorption using two different
              laser systems and was successfully used for ambient mass
              spectrometric imaging. 20 µm spatial resolution was achieved with
              oversampling, approaching single-cell resolution, while
              metabolites and lipids ranging from amino acids through
              carbohydrates and nuclear bases to complex glycolipids were
              successfully detected. The technique was also tested as a platform
              for MS-guided surgery, raising the possibility of using a single
