  volume   =  107,
  number   =  7,
  pages    = "2751--2756",
  abstract = "Mass spectrometry imaging has been used here to suggest that
              changes in membrane structure drive lipid domain formation in
              mating single-cell organisms. Chemical studies of lipid bilayers
              in both living and model systems have revealed that chemical
              composition is coupled to localized membrane structure. However,
              it is not clear if the lipids that compose the membrane actively
              modify membrane structure or if structural changes cause
              heterogeneity in the surface chemistry of the lipid bilayer. We
              report that time-of-flight secondary ion mass spectrometry images
              of mating Tetrahymena thermophila acquired at various stages
              during mating demonstrate that lipid domain formation, identified
