  volume   =  38,
  number   =  1,
  pages    = "62--69",
  abstract = "Lactobacillus bacteremia is a rare entity, and its clinical
              significance is poorly defined. We have reviewed the risk factors
              and outcome for 89 case patients with Lactobacillus bacteremia.
              Species characterization was done in 53\% of the cases, revealing
              25 L. rhamnosus strains and 22 other Lactobacillus species. In 11
              cases, the strain was identical with the probiotic L. rhamnosus
              GG. In 82\% of the cases, the patients had severe or fatal
              comorbidities. Predisposing factors to bacteremia were
              immunosuppression, prior prolonged hospitalization, and prior
              surgical interventions. No significant differences were observed
              in these predisposing factors or clinical features between
              patients with cases associated with the various Lactobacillus
              species, other than higher C-reactive protein values in patients
              with L. rhamnosus bacteremia. Mortality was 26\% at 1 month and
              was 48\% at 1 year. In multivariate analysis, severe underlying
              diseases were a significant predictor for mortality (odds ratio
              [OR], 15.8), whereas treatment with antimicrobials effective in
