  author    = "Gothard, David and Roberts, Scott J and Shakesheff, Kevin M and
               Buttery, Lee D K",
  journal   = "Cytotechnology",
  publisher = "Springer",
  volume    =  61,
  number    =  3,
  pages     = "135--144",
  abstract  = "Cell-cell interaction is an integral part of embryoid body (EB)
               formation controlling 3D aggregation. Manipulation of embryonic
               stem (ES) cell interactions could provide control over EB
               formation. Studies have shown a direct relationship between EB
               formation and ES cell differentiation. We have previously
               described a cell surface modification and cross-linking method
               for influencing cell-cell interaction and formation of
               multicellular constructs. Here we show further characterisation
               of this engineered aggregation. We demonstrate that engineering
               accelerates ES cell aggregation, forming larger, denser and more
               stable EBs than control samples, with no significant decrease in
               constituent ES cell viability. However, extended culture >/=5
               days reveals significant core necrosis creating a layered EB
