  author   = "Kang, Hyun-Jae and Kim, Jeong Ah and Lee, Hong Jai and Park, Tai
              Hyun",
  journal  = "Biomed. Microdevices",
  volume   =  11,
  number   =  1,
  pages    = "287--296",
  abstract = "A common problem with the in vivo therapeutic applications of
              cells is that cells can rapidly disappear into the circulatory
              system after an injection. Magnetic nanoparticles can be used to
              solve this problem. Bacterial magnetic nanoparticles were used in
              this study for targeting stem cells at a specific location within
              a microfluidic channel. Magnetic nanoparticles were isolated from
              Magnetospirillum sp. AMB-1 and delivered to endothelial progenitor
              cells (EPCs). Cellular uptake of magnetic nanoparticles and their
              functional feasibility was characterized in vitro. The environment
              of a human blood vessel was simulated using a microfluidic
              channel. Magnetic nanoparticle-incorporated EPCs were injected
              into a microchannel and the flow rate of cells was uniformly
