              predicting cryobiological outcomes. The author has been guided by
              a few core principles in using these concepts and tools and this
              paper discusses these core principles. They are (1) the importance
              of chemical potentials and of the difference between the chemical
              potentials of water and solutes inside the cell and outside in
              determining the direction and rate of fluxes of water and solutes.
              (2) The influence of the curvature of an ice crystal on its
              chemical potential and on the ability of ice to pass through pores
              in cell membranes, on the nucleation temperature of supercooled
