               standard for phenotypic identification of each cell. We were able
               to discriminate CMs from other phenotypes with >97\% specificity
               and >96\% sensitivity, as calculated with the use of
               cross-validation algorithms (target 100\% specificity). A
               comparison between Raman spectral images corresponding to
               selected Raman bands identified by the PCA model and
               immunostaining of the same cells allowed assignment of the Raman
               spectral markers. We conclude that glycogen is responsible for
               the discrimination of CMs, whereas myofibril proteins have a
               lesser contribution. This study demonstrates the potential of RMS
               for allowing the noninvasive phenotypic identification of hESC
               progeny. With further development, such label-free optical
