  volume    =  294,
  number    =  5547,
  pages     = "1708--1712",
  abstract  = "Adhesions between fibroblastic cells and extracellular matrix
               have been studied extensively in vitro, but little is known about
               their in vivo counterparts. Here, we characterized the
               composition and function of adhesions in three-dimensional (3D)
               matrices derived from tissues or cell culture. ``3D-matrix
               adhesions'' differ from focal and fibrillar adhesions
               characterized on 2D substrates in their content of alpha5beta1
               and alphavbeta3 integrins, paxillin, other cytoskeletal
               components, and tyrosine phosphorylation of focal adhesion kinase
               (FAK). Relative to 2D substrates, 3D-matrix interactions also
