              and osteopontin expression in experimentally induced myocardial
              infarction in rats, in comparison with Type I collagen expression.
              Northern blotting demonstrated that osteonectin mRNA did not
              markedly increase on Day 2 after the infarction, but it increased
              on Days 7 and 14 by 1.7 F 0.12- and 1.8 F 0.01- fold compared to
              that in preligation hearts. In contrast, osteopontin mRNA was
              increased on Day 1 (41.9 F 11.3-fold increase) and on Day 2
              (58.3F7.6-fold increase), and then it declined on Days 7 and 14
              (24.8F9.0- and 13.5F4.7-fold increase, respectively). In situ
              hybridization revealed that osteonectin mRNA signals were observed
