  pages    = "298--305",
  abstract = "Superparamagnetic iron oxide nanoparticles (SPIONs) have attract a
              great deal of interest in biomedical research and clinical
              applications over the past decades. Taking advantage the fact that
              SPIONs only exhibit magnetic properties in the presence of an
              applied magnetic field, they have been used in both in vitro
              magnetic separation and in vivo applications such as hyperthermia
              (HT), magnetic drug targeting (MDT), magnetic resonance imaging
              (MRI), gene delivery (GD) and nanomedicine. Successful
              applications of SPIONs rely on precise control of the particle's
